Lab 19 Setup Explainer Using Analogy's 

Key-based login

🐕 The analogy: a password is a secret word you whisper to Mutley at the door. Alan's dodgy mates can stand outside all night shouting guesses. A key pair works
differently. Mutley holds a wax impression of your signet ring (the public key), and only the real ring (the private key) makes a seal that matches. The ring never
leaves your hand, so there's nothing to overhear or guess. Alan carves a fake ring out of a potato, and Mutley eats the potato and the finger holding it.
The technical point: with key authentication, the server sends a challenge that only your private key can sign. The private key never crosses the network,
which defeats password guessing, spraying and credential theft from the wire.


Turn off password login

🐕 Now that there is a signet ring, Mutley stops listening to whispered passwords at the door. Alan rocks up shouting "password123!" and Mutley doesn't even
lift his head. He just points a paw at the sign: RINGS ONLY.


Setting up the ufw firewall

🐕 Mutley gets a gate. Nobody comes in at all except people from your street (your home network) going to door 22 (SSH). Alan tries climbing in a side window, 
the shutter slams down, and he's left hanging there by his trousers.
The technical point: a default-deny inbound firewall blocks every port except the ones you deliberately open, so a service you forgot about isn't reachable from 
outside.

Instead of editing the main sshd_config file I put the settings in a new file starting with 10-. Explain why this is a better?

The main sshd_config belongs to the OS package. When an update ships a new version, edits can be overwritten, or the update stops to ask which version 
to keep. A separate file that's setup is left alone.

Everything that changed is in one small file with a clear name. Anyone checking the server, including myself in six months or an auditor, can see exactly 
what was hardened without comparing a 120-line file against the default.

The first value wins per setting, not per session. SSH reads all the files, and for each setting (such as PasswordAuthentication) it keeps the first value it 
finds and ignores any later ones. That's why 10- beats Ubuntu's 50-cloud-init.conf. Lower numbers are read first, meaning the first value wins.

Mutley Analogy: Mutley's rulebook comes from Head Office, and every few months they post out a fresh copy and thow away the old one, along with any notes Miss Silky 
scribbled in the margins. So she pins her own card to the front of the kennel: "RINGS ONLY. NO ROOT." Mutley reads the card first and follows it, whatever the Head
Office book writes further along in the pages. Alan once tried slipping a "passwords OK" note into the back of the book, but Mutley had already read the front card,
so the note just went soggy in his mouth. The technical point: drop-in config files keep your hardening separate from the vendor's file, so it survives updates, 
is easy to audit, and takes priority when it's read first.

Why it matters at GRC level: this is configuration management in practice. My security baseline is a small, documented, reviewable change instead of an edit buried
in a vendor file. It's the kind of evidence an auditor asks for under "how do you know your servers stay hardened?"

