Lab 19: Network Adapter Setup 

Need to enable Network Adapter : this is the virtual network card. Unticked, the VM has no network at all.
 VirtualBox – Splunk01 Settings → Network → Adapter 1

Attached to: NAT Network: decides which "cable" the virtual card plugs into. The options:
NAT: each VM gets its own private bubble. It can reach the internet, but VMs can't see each other. That's no good here, because Splunk01 needs to hear from DC01.
NAT Network : a shared private network for several VMs, with a way out to the internet. That's why DC01, Win11-Ent-Lab and now Splunk01 are all on it.
Bridged Adapter: puts the VM straight onto your home network, next to your phone and the router. It's less isolated, so we avoid it for lab machines.
Internal Network: VMs can only talk to each other, with no internet.
Host-only Adapter: the VM can only talk to the laptop itself.
Name: LabNet: which NAT Network it joins. It must match DC01's setting exactly, or they end up on different networks.
Adapter Type: the brand of virtual card Linux sees. Left at the default Intel PRO/1000 MT Desktop. Ubuntu has drivers for it built in.
Promiscuous Mode: Deny: stops this VM from listening in on other VMs' traffic. Leave it on Deny. The only reason to change it would be for packet-sniffing labs.
MAC Address: the card's unique hardware ID. Has been left as is.  Would only change it when cloning VMs so two don't clash.
Cable Connected is ticked: a virtual "network cable plugged in". Unticking it is like pulling the cable out, which is handy for isolating a VM in an incident-response lab.
Port Forwarding: may be greyed out here, which is normal. For a NAT Network, port forwarding is set in Network Manager, not on the VM. That's the C4 step where I would let Windows Terminal reach 
Splunk01 for SSH and the Splunk web page.


The analogy: LabNet is the castle's private courtyard. DC01, Win11-Ent-Lab and Splunk01 all live inside the walls and can shout to each other. The gate (VirtualBox's NAT router at .1) lets them go out to the 
market (the internet), but strangers from outside can't wander in. Bridged would be like moving Mutley's kennel out onto the main road. Alan once did exactly that, and the kennel got hit by a bus.
The technical point: NAT Network gives the lab VMs one shared subnet with outbound internet access, and keeps them isolated from your home LAN unless you deliberately add port forwards.

Pause during setup - Explanation of what's happening

When Ubuntu starts for the first time, a setup tool called cloud-init runs in the background. It prints messages straight over the top of the login prompt: the SSH host key fingerprints, then a line 
saying it's finished. The splunk01 login: prompt is still there, just buried under all that text.

The analogy: Mutley has been let into his new kennel. Before he settles down, the castle clerk shouts out his new ID tags (the host key fingerprints) for the record. The clerk shouts right over the top of the
"who goes there?" sign on the door. The sign is still there, you just can't see it.

The technical point: on first boot, cloud-init creates the server's SSH host keys and prints their fingerprints to the console, which overwrites the login prompt on screen.
Pressing Enter redraws the prompt.
These fingerprints show again when you SSH in for the first time.

If this ever happens just click inside the VM window and press Enter once for a fresh splunk01 login: line appears at the bottom.


Check of avx - Unexpected Command Responce 

After Slunk Installed I ran this command on the VM.

grep -c avx /proc/cpuinfo

The grep command came back as 0 unexpected I didn't expect this I was expecting a return of maybe 2.  The Laptop's processor (a 10th-gen Core i7) definitely has AVX, so the hardware is fine. 
Something on Windows is hiding AVX from the VM.

The most likely cause: Windows has its own hypervisor (Hyper-V) running in the background. Features like Memory Integrity (Core Isolation), WSL or Virtual Machine Platform switch it on.
When it's on, VirtualBox can't reach the CPU directly. It has to run on top of Hyper-V instead, and in that mode the VM often doesn't get AVX.

The analogy: VirtualBox used to walk straight into the CPU's workshop. Now a second bouncer, Hyper-V, stands at the door. He searches everyone and confiscates the fancy power tools (AVX),
so the VM only gets a screwdriver. Alan the Fool once argued with a bouncer like that and left with his toolbelt stapled to his forehead.

The technical point: when Windows' hypervisor is active, VirtualBox runs in "native API" (NEM) mode on top of Hyper-V, and AVX/AVX2 are often not passed through to the guest.

Everything is fine, it's not yet the end of the world. 

